A stack can do the same job without storing separate level lists. Nodes are pushed right first, then left, so the right side is popped first. The `seenDepth` check makes sure only the first node at each depth is recorded.

One edge case is a tree made only of left children. It still works because when no right child exists, the left child becomes the first node reached at the next depth.

```java
import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.Deque;
import java.util.List;

/**
 * Definition for a binary tree node.
 * public class TreeNode {
 *     int data;
 *     TreeNode left;
 *     TreeNode right;
 *     TreeNode(int val) { data = val; left = null, right = null }
 * }
 **/

class Solution {
    public List<Integer> rightSideView(TreeNode root) {
        List<Integer> answer = new ArrayList<>();
        if (root == null) {
            return answer;
        }

        Deque<TreeNode> nodeStack = new ArrayDeque<>();
        Deque<Integer> depthStack = new ArrayDeque<>();
        nodeStack.push(root);
        depthStack.push(0);

        int seenDepth = 0;

        while (!nodeStack.isEmpty()) {
            TreeNode node = nodeStack.pop();
            int depth = depthStack.pop();

            if (depth == seenDepth) {
                answer.add(node.data);
                seenDepth++;
            }

            if (node.left != null) {
                nodeStack.push(node.left);
                depthStack.push(depth + 1);
            }
            if (node.right != null) {
                nodeStack.push(node.right);
                depthStack.push(depth + 1);
            }
        }

        return answer;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`, with one stack operation per node.
Space complexity: `O(h)` for the stacks in a balanced tree, and `O(n)` for a completely skewed tree.

Instead of remembering the last node in a level, this queue is filled from right to left. That makes the first node of each level the one to add to the answer.

The index `levelEnd` separates the current level from the nodes added for the next level. This avoids removing items from the front of a slice repeatedly.

```go
/*
Definition for a binary tree node.
type TreeNode struct {
    Val int
    Left *TreeNode
    Right *TreeNode
}
*/

func rightSideView(root *TreeNode) []int {
    if root == nil {
        return []int{}
    }

    answer := []int{}
    queue := []*TreeNode{root}
    levelStart := 0

    for levelStart < len(queue) {
        levelEnd := len(queue)
        answer = append(answer, queue[levelStart].Val)

        for i := levelStart; i < levelEnd; i++ {
            node := queue[i]

            if node.Right != nil {
                queue = append(queue, node.Right)
            }
            if node.Left != nil {
                queue = append(queue, node.Left)
            }
        }

        levelStart = levelEnd
    }

    return answer
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the queue in the widest case. The input tree is not changed.

The recursive version visits the right child before the left child. When a depth is reached for the first time, that node is the visible one for that depth.

### Small dry run

For this tree:

```text
    1
   / \
  2   3
       \
        4
```

The visit order starts as `1, 3, 4`. Those are the first nodes found at depths `0, 1, 2`, so the result becomes `[1, 3, 4]`. Node `2` is later at depth 1 and is ignored.

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

/**
 * Definition for a binary tree node.
 * public class TreeNode {
 *     int data;
 *     TreeNode left;
 *     TreeNode right;
 *     TreeNode(int val) { data = val; left = null, right = null }
 * }
 **/

class Solution {
    public List<Integer> rightSideView(TreeNode root) {
        List<Integer> answer = new ArrayList<>();
        collect(root, 0, answer);
        return answer;
    }

    private void collect(TreeNode node, int depth, List<Integer> answer) {
        if (node == null) {
            return;
        }

        if (depth == answer.size()) {
            answer.add(node.data);
        }

        collect(node.right, depth + 1, answer);
        collect(node.left, depth + 1, answer);
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(h)` for the recursion stack, where `h` is the tree height. The returned list also stores one value per level.

A level-by-level queue makes this pretty direct. The queue keeps nodes from left to right, so the last node processed in each level is the one visible from the right.

For example, with levels `[1]`, `[2, 3]`, and `[5, 4]`, the selected values are `1`, `3`, and `4`.

```cpp
/**
 * Definition for a binary tree node.
 * struct TreeNode {
 *     int data;
 *     TreeNode *left;
 *     TreeNode *right;
 *      TreeNode(int val) : data(val) , left(nullptr) , right(nullptr) {}
 * };
 **/

class Solution {
public:
    vector<int> rightSideView(TreeNode* root) {
        vector<int> answer;
        if (root == nullptr) {
            return answer;
        }

        queue<TreeNode*> nodes;
        nodes.push(root);

        while (!nodes.empty()) {
            int levelSize = nodes.size();

            for (int i = 0; i < levelSize; ++i) {
                TreeNode* current = nodes.front();
                nodes.pop();

                if (i == levelSize - 1) {
                    answer.push_back(current->data);
                }

                if (current->left != nullptr) {
                    nodes.push(current->left);
                }
                if (current->right != nullptr) {
                    nodes.push(current->right);
                }
            }
        }

        return answer;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`, because every node is visited once.
Space complexity: `O(w)`, where `w` is the largest level size. In the worst case this is `O(n)`.

